window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("ml-fundamentals", {"topics":[{"key":"ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers","sources":["Loshchilov & Hutter, 2017 — SGDR: Stochastic Gradient Descent with Warm Restarts","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Hu et al., 2024 — MiniCPM: Unveiling the Potential of Small Language Models with Scalable Training Strategies","Official docs: PyTorch torch.optim.lr_scheduler"],"why":"Stochastic gradient descent does not converge to a point with a constant learning rate; it converges\nto a *region*. Each mini-batch gradient is the true gradient plus noise, and the update `θ ← θ − η·g`\ncarries that noise into the weights. In a quadratic bowl the iterates end up bouncing around the minimum\nwith a spread proportional to `η × noise variance`. Halve η and the spread shrinks; the only way to\nactually reach the bottom is to make η small eventually.\n\nBut a small η from the start is wasteful. Early in training the gradient is large and consistently\npointed, and the model is far from any minimum, so a small step just takes many more iterations to cover\nthe same distance. Worse, a very small learning rate can leave the model stuck in a poor flat region that\na bigger step would have carried it past. Adaptive optimizers such as Adam do not remove this tension:\nAdam normalizes step sizes per parameter, but the global scale is still η, and the same noise-floor\nargument applies.\n\nThe naive fix, \"pick a medium η and train longer\", pays twice: you lose the fast early progress of a high\nη and still never get the low final loss of a small η. Schedulers resolve the conflict by making the\nlearning rate a function of time. They also solve a second, less obvious problem: at the very beginning\nAdam's running estimates of gradient variance are built from a handful of samples and are unreliable, so\nthe first few hundred steps need a *smaller* learning rate than the main phase. That ramp is warmup,\ncovered in its own lesson; this lesson is about what happens after it.","how":"A scheduler is a function `η(t)` of the optimizer step `t`, given a peak value `η_max`, a warmup length\nand a total length `T`. Frameworks implement it as a multiplier applied to the optimizer's base rate once\nper optimizer step. The common shapes:\n\n### Step decay\nMultiply η by a factor (often 0.1) at fixed milestones. It is the oldest recipe, from image classification,\nand it works because each plateau lets the model settle at one noise level before the next drop. Its\nweakness is that the milestones are hyperparameters chosen by hand, and the abrupt drops produce visible\ncliffs in the loss curve that make runs hard to compare.\n\n### Linear decay\n`η(t) = η_max × (1 − t/T)` after warmup. It decays to zero at exactly `T`. This is the default in the\nHugging Face `Trainer` and is the usual choice for fine-tuning, because fine-tuning runs are short, the\ntotal number of steps is known, and reaching a very small η at the end is what makes the final weights\nstable.\n\n### Cosine decay\n`η(t) = η_min + 0.5 × (η_max − η_min) × (1 + cos(π·t/T))`. It spends longer near `η_max` than linear\ndecay, then drops fastest in the middle and flattens again near `η_min`. The smooth tail is why it became\nthe standard for LLM pre-training; many published recipes decay to about 10% of the peak rather than\nto zero so the last phase still learns. The catch is that `T` must be fixed in advance: the shape is\nanchored to the end point, and training past `T` or stopping early both produce a different, usually\nworse, result than planning the right `T` from the start.\n\n### Inverse square root\nThe original Transformer used `η(t) ∝ min(t^−0.5, t·warmup^−1.5)`, i.e. linear warmup followed by\n`1/√t` decay. It needs no total length, which is convenient, but it decays slowly and never gets small\nenough for the final-loss benefits of an explicit anneal.\n\n### Warmup–stable–decay (WSD)\nHold `η_max` constant for most of the run, then decay sharply (linearly or cosine-like) over the final\n10–20% of steps. Because the stable phase has no end point baked in, you can continue training, or branch\na checkpoint from the stable phase and run a short decay to get a \"finished\" model at any point. Work\non MiniCPM and related scaling studies showed that this matches cosine at the same total steps while\nremoving the need to commit to `T` up front, which is why newer pre-training recipes often use it.\n\n### Cyclical and warm restarts\nSGDR restarts the cosine cycle periodically, jumping η back up. The intuition is that a jump kicks the\nmodel out of a sharp basin into a wider one; the practical benefit is cheap snapshot ensembles, one per\ncycle. It is rare in LLM work but common in smaller vision and tabular models.\n\n### What the scheduler steps on\nSchedulers advance per *optimizer* step, not per micro-batch and not per epoch. With gradient accumulation\nof 8, one scheduler step happens every 8 forward/backward passes. If `T` is computed from the number of\nmicro-batches, the schedule finishes 8× too early and the model trains most of the run at η ≈ 0.","example":"Linear warmup followed by cosine decay to 10% of peak, written as a plain `LambdaLR` so the arithmetic\nis visible. The printout checks the shape at a few known points: step 0 is zero, the end of warmup is the\npeak, and halfway through the decay phase the multiplier is exactly the midpoint between 1.0 and 0.1.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch\n\npeak_lr, warmup, total, floor = 3e-4, 100, 1000, 0.1\n\ndef multiplier(step):\n    if step < warmup:\n        return step / warmup                       # linear ramp 0 -> 1\n    progress = (step - warmup) / max(1, total - warmup)\n    cosine = 0.5 * (1 + math.cos(math.pi * progress))   # 1 -> 0\n    return floor + (1 - floor) * cosine            # 1 -> floor\n\nparams = [torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(10))]\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(params, lr=peak_lr)\nsched = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.LambdaLR(opt, multiplier)\n\nfor step in range(total):\n    # loss.backward(); opt.step()  -- one optimizer step per scheduler step\n    opt.step(); sched.step()\n    if step in (0, 99, 550, 999):\n        print(step, f\"{opt.param_groups[0]['lr']:.2e}\")\n# 0    -> 3.00e-06   (1/100 of the way up the ramp)\n# 99   -> 3.00e-04   (end of warmup: peak)\n# 550  -> 1.65e-04   (midpoint of decay: 0.1 + 0.9*0.5 = 0.55 of peak)\n# 999  -> 3.00e-05   (floor: 10% of peak)\n```\n\nThe same shape is available as `transformers.get_cosine_schedule_with_warmup`, and the WSD variant as\n`get_wsd_schedule`; the hand-written version is here so the numbers can be checked by eye.","tradeoffs":["Cosine and linear decay give the lowest final loss for a fixed budget, but they require knowing the total step count in advance; the moment training length is uncertain, WSD or a constant-then-cooldown schedule is the better trade.","A higher floor (decay to 10% rather than 0) keeps the model learning to the end and makes continued pre-training easier, at the cost of a slightly noisier final checkpoint than a full anneal.","Warm restarts and cyclical schedules buy ensemble diversity and sometimes escape sharp minima, but each restart costs a temporary loss increase that a short fine-tuning run cannot afford.","Inverse-square-root needs no horizon and is robust to running longer, but its slow tail leaves loss on the table compared with an explicit decay phase of the same length."],"pitfalls":["Calling `scheduler.step()` once per epoch with a per-step schedule (or vice versa); the symptom is a learning rate that is either stuck at the warmup value or collapses to near zero within the first epoch.","Computing `num_training_steps` from micro-batches when gradient accumulation is on, so the schedule finishes early and the second half of the run trains at a learning rate close to zero.","Resuming from a checkpoint without restoring scheduler state, which restarts warmup or resets the decay; the loss curve shows a jump at the resume point that was not there before.","Extending a cosine run past its planned end: the learning rate is already at the floor, so the extra steps barely move the model, and people conclude \"more data did not help\".","Reading the sharp loss drop during the decay phase as a sign the model suddenly \"got it\"; the drop is the noise floor shrinking as η falls, and it appears in every well-scheduled run."],"when":"Every real training run has a schedule; the decision is which one. For fine-tuning, use linear or cosine\ndecay with a short warmup and let the run end at a small learning rate, because the total steps are known\nand stability of the final weights matters more than squeezing out the last fraction of loss. For\npre-training or long continued pre-training where the budget may change, prefer WSD or constant with a\nplanned cooldown so checkpoints remain useful at any point. Before launching, print the learning rate at\nsteps 0, end of warmup, midpoint and end, and check they match the plan; log the learning rate alongside\nloss so a bad curve can be attributed. In interviews, the expected depth is being able to explain *why*\ndecay lowers final loss (noise floor), why the schedule must be tied to optimizer steps, and when cosine's\nfixed horizon is a liability.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `lr_scheduler_type`, `warmup_ratio` and `num_training_steps` in the training config, and verifies the schedule by logging the learning rate rather than trusting defaults.","architect":"Decides the training-budget policy: whether runs have fixed horizons (cosine) or must be extendable and checkpoint-branchable (WSD), which shapes the data pipeline and evaluation cadence.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"fine-tuning plateaued\" complaint by inspecting the schedule first, since a mis-set step count or a missing decay explains most such reports before touching the data."},"check":{"q":"A team fine-tunes with cosine decay over 3,000 steps, then decides the model is undertrained and\nruns 3,000 more steps by resuming the checkpoint with the same config. The extra steps barely change the\nloss. What happened, and what should they have done?","a":"The resumed run restored the scheduler at step 3,000, where cosine has already reached its floor,\nso all 3,000 extra steps ran at the minimum learning rate and the weights hardly moved. The result says\nnothing about whether more data helps. Options: restart from the original checkpoint with `T = 6,000`\nso the schedule is planned for the full length; or, if resuming is required, create a new schedule that\nre-warms briefly to a moderate peak and decays over the extra steps. Going forward, a WSD-style schedule\nwould let them extend the stable phase and apply the decay only once the final length is known. The\ngeneral lesson is that the schedule is a function of planned total steps, so changing the plan means\nchanging the schedule."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does decaying the learning rate late in training usually lower the final loss?","options":["It reduces the model's capacity, which acts as regularization","Mini-batch gradient noise makes the weights bounce in a region whose size scales with the learning rate, so a smaller rate lets them settle closer to the minimum","It forces the optimizer to revisit earlier examples more often","It compensates for Adam's second-moment estimate becoming too large"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SGD with a constant rate converges to a noise-limited region, not a point; shrinking η shrinks that region. Capacity is unchanged, and Adam's second moment is a separate per-parameter scaling."},{"q":"A run uses gradient accumulation of 4 and the schedule's total steps were set to the number of micro-batches. What goes wrong?","options":["The learning rate peaks at 4× the intended value","Warmup takes 4× longer than intended","The schedule completes after a quarter of the run, so most training happens at the floor learning rate","Nothing, because schedulers count micro-batches internally"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Schedulers advance once per optimizer step, and with accumulation of 4 there are a quarter as many optimizer steps as micro-batches, so a schedule sized in micro-batches reaches its end four times too early."},{"q":"Which schedule lets you branch a usable \"finished\" model from a checkpoint partway through a long pre-training run?","options":["Cosine decay to zero","Step decay with fixed milestones","Warmup–stable–decay, by running a short decay from a stable-phase checkpoint","Inverse square root, because it has no horizon"],"answer":2,"explanation":"WSD keeps the learning rate constant for most of the run, so any stable-phase checkpoint can be annealed with a short decay to get the final-loss benefit. Cosine's shape is anchored to a fixed end point, and inverse-square-root never anneals sharply."}]},{"key":"ml-fundamentals/warmup","sources":["Goyal et al., 2017 — Accurate, Large Minibatch SGD: Training ImageNet in 1 Hour","Liu et al., 2020 — On the Variance of the Adaptive Learning Rate and Beyond","Xiong et al., 2020 — On Layer Normalization in the Transformer Architecture"],"why":"At initialization nothing about the network is calibrated. Activations have whatever scale the init gave\nthem, the loss is at its maximum, and the gradient is large in magnitude and dominated by a few\ndirections (fix the output bias, shrink everything) rather than by anything useful. Taking a full\nlearning-rate step here moves the weights a long way along a direction computed from a landscape that\nwill look completely different one step later. The visible result is a loss that drops for a few steps,\nthen spikes, then either recovers slowly or produces NaNs.\n\nAdaptive optimizers make this worse in a specific way. Adam divides each parameter's update by the square\nroot of a running average of squared gradients, `√v̂`. After one step, `v` is an estimate from a single\nsample; after ten, from ten. The variance of that estimate is enormous early, and a parameter whose few\nobserved gradients happened to be small gets an update that is far too large. The RAdam analysis made this\nprecise: the effective per-parameter step size has divergent variance in the first iterations, and a\nlearning-rate warmup is a cheap way to keep updates small until `v` has enough samples to be trusted.\n\nArchitecture adds a third reason. In the original Post-LN transformer, the gradient norm near the output\nlayers at initialization grows with depth, so the first updates are disproportionately large there; the\npaper that analysed this showed Post-LN is unstable without warmup, while Pre-LN tolerates its absence.\nLarge-batch training is a fourth: a bigger batch justifies a bigger learning rate, but only once the\nmodel has left the chaotic initial region, so the ImageNet-in-one-hour recipe ramped the rate linearly\nover the first five epochs rather than starting at the scaled peak.\n\nThe naive alternative is simply a lower peak learning rate. It avoids the early blow-up, but the whole run\nthen trains too slowly and the final loss is worse. Warmup separates the two concerns: a conservative\nstart and an aggressive middle.","how":"Warmup is a multiplier on the learning rate for the first `W` optimizer steps, after which the main\nschedule takes over:\n\n```\nη(t) = η_max × t / W           for t < W      (linear warmup)\nη(t) = main_schedule(t)        for t ≥ W\n```\n\nLinear is standard; some recipes start from a small non-zero fraction instead of exactly zero so the\nvery first step still does something. The ramp is in *optimizer steps*, which matters with gradient\naccumulation: 500 warmup steps with accumulation 8 means 4,000 micro-batches.\n\n### What it protects against, step by step\n1. **Steps 1–10.** Adam's `m` and `v` are nearly empty. Bias correction fixes their *mean* but not their\n   *variance*; the per-parameter step `m̂/√v̂` can be wildly off for individual weights. A tiny η keeps\n   those mis-scaled updates harmless while `v` fills.\n2. **Steps 10–W.** Gradients are still large and the loss surface is sharp. Weight norms, LayerNorm\n   statistics and attention logit scales are settling. A rising η lets the model take progressively larger\n   steps as the curvature along the trajectory decreases.\n3. **After W.** The model is in a smoother basin, moment estimates are reliable, and the main schedule\n   (cosine, linear, WSD) governs.\n\n### How long\nThere is no closed form. Practical ranges: for fine-tuning, a few percent of total steps or a fixed 50–200\nsteps; for pre-training, on the order of a thousand to a few thousand steps (Llama 2, for example,\nreports 2,000). Larger peak learning rates, larger batches and Post-LN architectures want longer warmup;\nsmall fine-tuning runs with a low peak often work with very little. The tell for \"too short\" is a loss\nspike or a sudden rise in gradient norm within the first few hundred steps; the tell for \"too long\" is\na flat early loss curve that only starts dropping when the ramp ends.\n\n### Interaction with other stabilizers\nWarmup, gradient clipping and Pre-LN normalization all attack the same early instability from different\nsides, which is why modern recipes use all three. Clipping bounds the damage of any single bad step;\nPre-LN keeps gradient magnitudes roughly uniform across depth; warmup shrinks the step itself. Removing\none usually means asking more of the others.\n\n### Resuming and re-warming\nWhen resuming a run from a checkpoint with intact optimizer state, warmup should *not* repeat, because\n`v` is already populated; the scheduler state must be restored so the learning rate continues where it\nleft off. When starting a new phase (continued pre-training on new data, or fine-tuning from a base model)\nthe optimizer state is fresh, so a new warmup is needed even though the weights are trained.","example":"A fine-tuning configuration where the warmup length is derived from the real number of optimizer steps,\nthen checked by printing the learning rate at the ends of the ramp. The scheduler comes from\n`transformers`; the arithmetic that most people get wrong is the step count.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import get_cosine_schedule_with_warmup\n\nn_examples, epochs = 20_000, 3\nmicro_batch, accum, n_gpus = 8, 4, 2\nseq_per_opt_step = micro_batch * accum * n_gpus          # 64 sequences per optimizer step\nopt_steps = epochs * (n_examples // seq_per_opt_step)    # 3 * 312 = 936 optimizer steps\nwarmup = int(0.05 * opt_steps)                           # 5% -> 46 steps\n\nparams = [torch.nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(4))]\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(params, lr=2e-5)\nsched = get_cosine_schedule_with_warmup(opt, num_warmup_steps=warmup,\n                                        num_training_steps=opt_steps)\n\nseen = {}\nfor step in range(opt_steps):\n    # for _ in range(accum): loss.backward()   # accumulate micro-batches\n    opt.step(); opt.zero_grad(); sched.step()  # ONE scheduler step per optimizer step\n    seen[step] = opt.param_groups[0][\"lr\"]\n\nprint(f\"{seen[0]:.2e} {seen[warmup // 2]:.2e} {seen[warmup - 1]:.2e} {seen[opt_steps - 1]:.2e}\")\n# ~4.3e-07  ~1.0e-05  2.0e-05  ~0  -> ramp reaches the peak at step 45, then cosine decays\n```\n\nIf `opt_steps` had been computed from micro-batches (`epochs * n_examples // micro_batch` = 7,500), the\nsame 5% rule would give 375 warmup steps, longer than the entire real run of 936 optimizer steps would\nneed, and the cosine decay would never reach its floor.","tradeoffs":["A longer warmup is safer against early divergence but spends more of a short fine-tuning run below the productive learning rate; past roughly 10% of total steps the safety gain is usually negligible.","Warmup lets you use a higher peak learning rate (and therefore reach a given loss in fewer steps), but the higher peak then needs a proper decay phase or the run ends noisy.","Pre-LN architectures and gradient clipping reduce the *need* for warmup, so very short ramps work, but removing warmup entirely still risks polluting Adam's moment estimates with the first erratic gradients.","Skipping warmup on resume is correct when optimizer state is restored, and wrong when it is not; the convenience of \"just reload the weights\" trades for a second unstable start."],"pitfalls":["Setting `warmup_steps` as if steps were micro-batches or epochs; the symptom is either a ramp that never finishes before the run ends or one that is over after a single logging interval.","Resuming a crashed run without the scheduler state, so warmup restarts from zero at step 10,000; the loss curve shows a visible dip in progress for the next few hundred steps.","Treating a loss spike at step 50 as a data problem when it is a warmup problem; check the gradient norm trace first, which jumps before the loss does.","Reusing a pre-training warmup length (thousands of steps) for a fine-tuning run of 500 steps, so the model never reaches its intended learning rate.","Starting LoRA fine-tuning at a high peak rate with no warmup; the adapters are zero-initialized and the first few updates can set a bad direction that later steps only partially undo."],"when":"Use warmup in essentially every run that uses Adam-family optimizers, which is every run in modern\nAI engineering. For fine-tuning, default to a few percent of total optimizer steps and verify the\nstep count. For pre-training or large-batch training, size it in the thousands of steps and increase it\nwhen raising the peak learning rate or batch size. Skip it only in tiny experiments where divergence is\ncheap to notice and restart. Measure it by logging the learning rate and gradient norm from step zero:\na healthy run shows the norm falling as the rate rises. In interviews, the question is rarely \"what is\nwarmup\" but \"why does Adam need it\", and the answer is the variance of early second-moment estimates\nplus large, poorly conditioned initial gradients.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes warmup from the real optimizer step count, logs learning rate and grad norm from step zero, and reads early loss spikes as a warmup or clipping problem before blaming data.","architect":"Sets the stability recipe for the organization's training stack (warmup length, clipping, Pre-LN) so that teams scaling batch or learning rate do not rediscover divergence independently.","fde":"When a customer's fine-tuning job diverges on their own data, checks warmup and step accounting in their config first, since a mis-sized ramp is the most common cause and the cheapest fix."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues that because the model uses Pre-LN and gradient clipping at 1.0, warmup is\nredundant and they will fine-tune at a constant 1e-4 from step one. What do you expect to happen, and why?","a":"Pre-LN removes the depth-dependent gradient blow-up and clipping bounds the size of any one step,\nbut neither fixes Adam's early second-moment estimates. In the first steps `v` is estimated from one or\ntwo gradients per parameter, so individual parameters whose first observed gradients were small receive\nupdates far larger than intended even after clipping, because clipping acts on the raw gradient, not on\nAdam's normalized step. Expect a loss that drops briefly and then spikes within the first hundred steps,\nor a run that converges but to a worse final loss than the same recipe with a 50-step ramp. The cost of\nwarmup is a few percent of a short run, and the fix for any observed spike is cheaper up front than after\na wasted run."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Adam specifically benefit from learning-rate warmup?","options":["Adam has no bias correction, so early updates are biased toward zero","Its per-parameter second-moment estimate is built from very few samples early on, so normalized step sizes have high variance until enough gradients have been seen","Adam's momentum term is zero at the start, so updates are too small without warmup","Adam requires the learning rate to be below the Lipschitz constant, which warmup estimates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bias correction fixes the mean of the moment estimates, not their variance; the early `v` is simply noisy. Warmup keeps the learning rate small while that estimate stabilizes."},{"q":"A run with gradient accumulation of 8 is configured with 400 warmup steps and 10,000 micro-batches in total. How much of the run is warmup?","options":["4%, because warmup is counted in micro-batches","32%, because there are only 1,250 optimizer steps and warmup counts those","0.4%, because warmup counts tokens","It depends on the number of GPUs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Schedulers advance per optimizer step. 10,000 micro-batches at accumulation 8 is 1,250 optimizer steps, so 400 warmup steps is nearly a third of the run, far longer than intended."},{"q":"You resume a crashed pre-training run from a checkpoint that includes optimizer state. Should warmup repeat?","options":["Yes, because the GPU memory state was lost","Yes, warmup is always required after any restart","No; the second-moment estimates are already populated, so the scheduler should continue from its saved step","Only if the learning rate at the crash point was above the warmup peak"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The purpose of warmup is to protect against unreliable early optimizer statistics and a random init. With optimizer state restored, neither applies, and repeating the ramp just slows the next few hundred steps."}]},{"key":"ml-fundamentals/gradient-clipping","sources":["Pascanu et al., 2013 — On the difficulty of training recurrent neural networks","Zhang et al., 2020 — Why Gradient Clipping Accelerates Training: A Theoretical Justification for Adaptivity","Official docs: PyTorch torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_"],"why":"Backpropagation multiplies Jacobians layer by layer. Whenever those products line up, the gradient at the\nbottom of the network can be orders of magnitude larger than typical. In recurrent networks this happened\nsystematically along the time axis, and the 2013 analysis of exploding gradients proposed clipping as the\nfix. In transformers it happens sporadically: an unusual batch, a sharp region of the loss surface, an\nattention pattern that saturates, or a numerical edge case in mixed precision produces a gradient norm of\n50 where the running average is 1.\n\nThe damage from one such step is out of proportion to its frequency. With plain SGD the weights jump by\n`η × 50` instead of `η × 1`, which can move the model out of the basin it spent a day finding. With Adam\nthe raw step is normalized, which softens the immediate jump, but the huge gradient enters the first and\nsecond moment estimates. The second moment `v` uses a decay like 0.95 or 0.999, so a single squared value\n2,500× larger than normal inflates `v` for hundreds of steps and shrinks every subsequent update for that\nparameter. The run does not crash; it quietly learns slower for a while, and the loss curve shows a\nplateau nobody can explain.\n\nThe naive alternative is to lower the learning rate until the worst batch is survivable. That penalizes\nevery normal step to protect against a rare one. Clipping is the targeted version: leave normal steps\nuntouched and bound only the outliers. Theory later caught up with practice: clipped gradient descent can\nbe shown to converge faster than fixed-step descent on functions whose smoothness varies with gradient\nmagnitude, which is the regime deep networks live in.","how":"### Global norm clipping\nCollect every parameter gradient, compute one scalar:\n\n```\ntotal_norm = sqrt( Σ_params Σ_elements g² )\nscale      = min(1, max_norm / (total_norm + ε))\ng         ← g × scale        for every parameter\n```\n\nTwo design choices matter. First, the norm is *global*, across all parameters, not per tensor. Per-tensor\nclipping would rescale layers by different factors and change the direction of the overall step; global\nclipping multiplies everything by the same scalar, so the step points exactly where backprop said and is\nmerely shorter. Second, the operation is a no-op when `total_norm ≤ max_norm`. On a healthy run clipping\nshould fire on a minority of steps; if it fires on almost every step, the threshold is acting as a\nconstant learning-rate reduction and the real problem is elsewhere, usually the peak learning rate or a\nmissing warmup.\n\n### Value clipping\n`clip_grad_value_` instead clamps each element to `[−c, c]`. It is cheaper to reason about but changes\nthe direction of the gradient, since large components are flattened while small ones are untouched. It\nsurvives mostly in older RNN code; transformer recipes use norm clipping, almost universally with\n`max_norm = 1.0`.\n\n### Where it sits in the step\nClipping must happen after the full gradient for the optimizer step exists and before the optimizer uses\nit:\n\n1. Run forward and backward for every micro-batch in the accumulation window; gradients sum in `.grad`.\n2. If using fp16 loss scaling, **unscale** the gradients first. The scaled gradients are thousands of\n   times larger than real, so clipping them at 1.0 would clip everything to nearly zero.\n3. Compute the global norm and clip. Log the pre-clip norm.\n4. `optimizer.step()`, then `zero_grad()`.\n\nClipping each micro-batch separately is wrong for the same reason per-tensor clipping is: the accumulated\ngradient is a sum, and clipping the pieces changes both its magnitude and its direction.\n\n### Distributed training\nUnder data parallelism the gradient is all-reduced before the step, so clipping the local gradient after\nthe reduction is equivalent to clipping the global one. Under sharded training (FSDP, ZeRO) each rank\nholds only part of the gradient, so the norm must be computed as a sum of squares across ranks followed by\na collective; sharding frameworks expose their own clipping function for this reason, and calling the\nplain PyTorch utility on sharded parameters silently computes the wrong norm.\n\n### The norm as a health signal\nThe pre-clip gradient norm is one of the most informative training curves. It should be large in the\nfirst steps, fall during warmup, then drift slowly. Spikes that coincide with loss spikes point at data or\nnumerics; a steady upward trend late in training points at the learning rate being too high for the\ncurrent loss surface; a norm that sits above the threshold for hundreds of consecutive steps means the\nrun is being throttled by the clip rather than protected by it.","example":"A training step with accumulation, fp16 loss scaling and clipping in the correct order. The function\nreturns the pre-clip norm so it can be logged, and the comment shows the arithmetic for one concrete\nstep.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom torch.nn.utils import clip_grad_norm_\n\ndef train_step(model, opt, scaler, micro_batches, accum, max_norm=1.0):\n    opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    for x, y in micro_batches:                       # len(micro_batches) == accum\n        with torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=torch.float16):\n            loss = torch.nn.functional.cross_entropy(model(x), y) / accum\n        scaler.scale(loss).backward()                # grads are scaled by S (e.g. 65536)\n    scaler.unscale_(opt)                             # divide grads by S BEFORE measuring the norm\n    pre_clip = clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), max_norm)   # returns the norm before clipping\n    scaler.step(opt)                                 # skips the step if any grad is inf/nan\n    scaler.update()\n    return pre_clip.item()\n\n# Worked step: suppose the accumulated gradient has global norm 4.0 and max_norm is 1.0.\n#   scale = min(1, 1.0 / 4.0) = 0.25 -> every element is multiplied by 0.25, direction unchanged.\n# If instead the norm had been 0.7, scale = min(1, 1/0.7) = 1 -> nothing happens.\n# Forgetting unscale_(): the scaled norm is 4.0 * 65536 = 262144, so the clip scales it to 1.0;\n#   when the scaler then unscales internally, the real gradient norm is 1/65536 ~ 1.5e-5 instead of 4.0.\n```\n\nWith bf16 there is no scaler; drop the `scaler` calls and call `clip_grad_norm_` directly after the last\n`backward()`. The ordering rule (accumulate, unscale, clip, step) is the same.","tradeoffs":["Clipping bounds the damage of rare outlier batches at no cost on normal steps, but if the threshold is far below the typical norm it becomes a hidden learning-rate cut that slows the whole run.","Global-norm clipping preserves update direction, while value clipping is simpler and cheaper but distorts the direction whenever a few coordinates dominate.","A tighter threshold buys stability for aggressive learning rates and large models, but it masks problems (bad data, numeric overflow) that would otherwise show up as a clear divergence.","Under sharded training the global norm requires an extra collective per step; the cost is small compared with the gradient all-reduce, but skipping it in favor of local norms produces subtly inconsistent clipping."],"pitfalls":["Clipping before `unscale_()` under fp16: every step is clipped to near zero and the loss barely moves while nothing reports an error.","Clipping after `optimizer.step()` or before `backward()`: the call succeeds, does nothing useful, and the first real gradient spike still corrupts the run.","Clipping each micro-batch in a gradient-accumulation loop, which changes the direction of the summed gradient and makes the effective threshold depend on the accumulation count.","Calling the plain PyTorch utility on FSDP-sharded parameters; the norm is computed over the local shard only, so clipping behaves differently on every rank.","Using clipping to paper over divergence caused by a too-high learning rate or missing warmup; the clip fires on nearly every step, which is the signal that the root cause is still there.","Not logging the pre-clip norm, which throws away the single best early-warning signal for bad data, numeric issues and a learning rate that has outlived its schedule."],"when":"Turn it on for every transformer training run; the default of `max_norm = 1.0` is a sensible starting\npoint, and training frameworks such as the Hugging Face `Trainer` already apply it. The decision is less\nwhether to clip than how to read it: log the pre-clip norm, note the fraction of steps on which clipping\nfired, and treat sustained clipping as a configuration problem rather than a success. Revisit the\nthreshold when changing optimizer, precision or batch size, since all three move the typical norm.\nIn interviews, the expected depth is the ordering (accumulate, unscale, clip, step), the reason global\nnorm beats per-element clipping, and why clipping still matters with Adam even though Adam normalizes\nstep sizes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Places the clip call correctly relative to loss scaling and accumulation, logs the pre-clip norm, and alerts on it as part of training-run monitoring.","architect":"Fixes clipping and norm-logging as part of the standard training recipe so that every team's runs produce comparable stability diagnostics across models and clusters.","fde":"When a customer's fine-tuning diverged or stalled, pulls their gradient-norm trace to show whether the run was unprotected or quietly throttled, turning a vague complaint into a concrete fix."},"check":{"q":"A team trains with Adam, warmup, bf16 and clipping at 1.0. Their logs show clipping fired on 97% of\nsteps for the entire run, yet the loss decreased steadily and nothing diverged. Is this fine?","a":"It is not a failure, but it is a sign that the configuration is not what they think it is. If\nthe norm is above 1.0 almost every step, clipping is acting as a constant rescaling of the gradient\nrather than a safety net, so the effective learning rate is lower than the configured one and varies\nwith the gradient norm from step to step. The run converged, but probably slower than it could have, and\nthe schedule they tuned is not the schedule that actually ran. The usual causes are a learning rate set\ntoo high for the model size, a loss that is summed instead of averaged over tokens so its scale is large,\nor a threshold copied from a different setup. The fix is to look at the pre-clip norm distribution and\neither raise the threshold to sit above the typical norm or correct the loss scaling and learning rate so\nclipping fires only on outliers."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is global-norm clipping preferred over clamping each gradient element to a fixed range?","options":["It is cheaper because it needs only one pass over the gradients","It multiplies every gradient by the same scalar, so the update keeps its direction and only its length is bounded","It works with Adam, whereas value clipping only works with SGD","It automatically adapts the threshold to the learning rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clamping elements flattens the large coordinates and leaves small ones alone, which changes where the step points. A single global scale factor shortens the step without rotating it."},{"q":"In an fp16 training loop with a GradScaler, where must the clipping call go?","options":["Immediately after the loss is computed, before backward","After `scaler.step()` so the optimizer has already applied the update","After `scaler.unscale_(optimizer)` and before `scaler.step()`","Anywhere, because the scaler accounts for clipping automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Gradients after backward are multiplied by the loss scale, so their norm is thousands of times too large. Unscaling first makes the threshold meaningful; clipping after the step is too late to affect it."},{"q":"Adam already normalizes each parameter's update by its running gradient magnitude. Why does clipping still help?","options":["It does not; clipping is only useful with SGD","Clipping replaces the need for a second-moment estimate","A single huge gradient still enters Adam's moment estimates and distorts updates for many subsequent steps","Adam's normalization only applies to the first layer of the network"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adam's second moment has a long memory, so one outlier inflates it and shrinks later updates for that parameter; clipping keeps the outlier out of the statistics in the first place."}]},{"key":"ml-fundamentals/batch-size","sources":["McCandlish et al., 2018 — An Empirical Model of Large-Batch Training","Goyal et al., 2017 — Accurate, Large Minibatch SGD: Training ImageNet in 1 Hour","Shallue et al., 2019 — Measuring the Effects of Data Parallelism on Neural Network Training","Keskar et al., 2017 — On Large-Batch Training for Deep Learning: Generalization Gap and Sharp Minima"],"why":"Full-batch gradient descent computes the exact gradient over the whole dataset once per step. For a\ndataset of billions of tokens that is absurd: one step would take hours and the model would learn nothing\nuntil it finished. Stochastic gradient descent instead estimates the gradient from a sample, and the\nbatch size is the sample size. That makes it a statistics question. The mini-batch gradient is an\nunbiased estimate of the full gradient with variance proportional to `1/B`. Small `B` gives cheap,\nnoisy steps; large `B` gives expensive, accurate ones.\n\nHardware pushes in one direction. A GPU multiplies matrices efficiently only when they are large, and\nthe batch dimension is what makes the activations large. A batch of one sequence leaves most of the chip\nidle and makes the step memory-bandwidth bound (weights are read once per step regardless of batch, so\nmore examples per step amortize that read). Doubling the batch until the GPU is compute-bound is close to\nfree in wall-clock per example. Data parallelism pushes the same way: each additional GPU usually adds\nits share of examples to the batch rather than splitting a fixed batch finer.\n\nOptimization pushes back. Once the gradient estimate is already accurate, more samples do not change the\ndirection of the step, so a batch twice as large produces essentially the same update and the model\nlearns the same amount per step from twice the data. The run needs the same number of steps but consumes\ntwice the tokens: wasted compute, and for a fixed dataset, fewer epochs' worth of unique signal. The naive\npolicy \"use the biggest batch that fits\" is therefore wrong in both directions: too small wastes hardware,\ntoo large wastes data. The useful question is where the crossover is, and the answer depends on the\nnoise in the gradient, which changes over training.","how":"### Gradient noise and the critical batch size\nWrite the per-example gradient as the true gradient `G` plus noise with covariance `Σ`. The *gradient\nnoise scale* is roughly `B_noise = tr(Σ) / |G|²`: the batch size at which the noise in the estimate is\ncomparable to the signal. The empirical model of large-batch training gives a clean relation between the\nsteps `S` and examples `E` a run needs at batch `B`, relative to the minimums `S_min` (infinite batch) and\n`E_min` (infinitesimal batch):\n\n```\nS = S_min × (1 + B_noise / B)        steps\nE = E_min × (1 + B / B_noise)        examples processed  (E = S × B)\n```\n\nWith `B ≪ B_noise`, doubling `B` roughly halves `S` for the same `E`: perfect scaling, free speed-up from\nmore hardware. With `B ≫ B_noise`, doubling `B` leaves `S` unchanged and doubles `E`: pure waste. At\n`B = B_noise` both are 2× their minimum, which is why the noise scale is a sensible \"critical\" batch size.\nTwo consequences follow. The noise scale *grows* during training, because `|G|` shrinks as the loss falls\nwhile the per-example noise does not, so the useful batch size is larger late in training than early;\nthis is why some pre-training recipes ramp the batch size up over the first part of the run. And harder\ntasks and larger models tend to have a larger noise scale, which is why LLM pre-training uses batches of\nmillions of tokens while a small classifier saturates at a few hundred examples.\n\n### Learning rate must move with batch size\nA bigger batch justifies a bigger step, because the step is computed from a more trustworthy gradient.\nFor SGD the *linear scaling rule* (multiply the learning rate by `k` when multiplying the batch by `k`)\nworks well in the perfect-scaling regime, provided the run has warmup; this is the recipe that trained\nImageNet in an hour at a batch of 8,192. For Adam-family optimizers the picture is less clean: square-root\nscaling is a common starting point with some theoretical support, but in practice the peak learning rate\nis re-tuned when the batch changes by more than a small factor. Either way, changing the batch without\ntouching the learning rate compares two different optimizers, not two batch sizes.\n\n### Generalization\nEarly work observed that very large batches reached sharper minima and generalized worse. Later, larger\nstudies showed most of that gap closes when the learning rate, warmup and number of steps are re-tuned\nfor the larger batch; what remains is the hard ceiling from the noise scale, not a mysterious property of\nbig batches. The practical reading: a large batch is not harmful by itself, but copying a small-batch\nrecipe to a large batch is.\n\n### Memory and the effective batch\nActivation memory scales linearly with the batch (and with sequence length), while weight, gradient and\noptimizer memory do not. On a given GPU the largest *micro-batch* that fits is a hardware fact; the\n*effective batch* that the optimizer sees is\n\n```\neffective_batch = micro_batch × gradient_accumulation_steps × data_parallel_world_size\n```\n\nand for LLMs it is usually quoted in tokens: `effective_batch × sequence_length`. Gradient accumulation\nand more GPUs change the effective batch without changing per-GPU memory; gradient checkpointing and\nmixed precision change how large the micro-batch can be. These are different knobs for different\nconstraints, and conflating them (\"I added GPUs so I can raise the learning rate\" is right; \"I added\naccumulation so training is faster\" is wrong) is a common source of confusion.\n\n### Inference is a different problem\nBatching requests at serving time shares the name but not the trade-off: it is about amortizing weight\nreads across concurrent users, with latency rather than gradient noise as the cost. That lives in the\nserving module under continuous batching.","example":"A planning function that turns a hardware constraint and a token budget into the three numbers that\nactually get written into a config, with the learning-rate adjustment made explicit. The numbers are\nillustrative; the structure is what matters.\n\n```python\ndef plan(tokens_budget, seq_len, target_batch_tokens,\n         micro_batch_per_gpu, n_gpus, base_lr, base_batch_tokens, rule=\"sqrt\"):\n    seqs_per_step = target_batch_tokens // seq_len\n    per_micro_step = micro_batch_per_gpu * n_gpus\n    accum = max(1, seqs_per_step // per_micro_step)\n    eff_tokens = accum * per_micro_step * seq_len          # what the optimizer actually sees\n    steps = tokens_budget // eff_tokens\n    ratio = eff_tokens / base_batch_tokens\n    lr = base_lr * (ratio ** 0.5 if rule == \"sqrt\" else ratio)\n    return dict(accum=accum, eff_tokens=eff_tokens, steps=steps, lr=lr)\n\nprint(plan(tokens_budget=10_000_000_000, seq_len=4096, target_batch_tokens=1_000_000,\n           micro_batch_per_gpu=4, n_gpus=8, base_lr=3e-4, base_batch_tokens=524_288))\n# -> accum 7, eff_tokens 917504, steps 10899, lr ~3.97e-4\n#   244 seqs/step wanted, 32 per micro-step -> 7 accumulation steps -> 224 seqs = 0.92M tokens\n#   10B tokens / 0.92M = ~10.9k optimizer steps; sqrt-scaling from a 0.5M-token recipe at 3e-4\n#   gives ~4.0e-4 (linear scaling would give 5.25e-4; either is a starting point to re-tune).\n```\n\nThe same function with `n_gpus=64` returns `accum=1` and `eff_tokens=1,048,576`: the target batch is now\nreached without accumulation, the step count drops to ~9.5k, and the learning rate moves only slightly\n(to ~4.2e-4) because the effective batch moved only slightly. That is the point: adding GPUs changed\nthroughput, not the optimization problem, because the plan held the effective batch roughly fixed.","tradeoffs":["Larger batches raise GPU utilization and cut wall-clock per token, but past the noise scale they stop reducing the step count, so each extra example is paid for in data and compute without a loss benefit.","Small batches give more optimizer steps per token and a regularizing noise, which helps on small datasets, but they under-use accelerators and make the step bandwidth-bound rather than compute-bound.","Scaling the learning rate with the batch keeps the optimizer comparable across hardware sizes, yet the exact rule (linear, square-root, re-tune) depends on the optimizer and breaks down near the noise scale.","A batch-size ramp matches the growing noise scale during training and saves data early, at the cost of a schedule whose step count and learning rate must be tied to tokens rather than steps.","Reaching a large effective batch through accumulation costs nothing in memory but delivers no speed-up; reaching it through more GPUs costs hardware but actually shortens the run."],"pitfalls":["Doubling the batch (or the GPU count) and leaving the learning rate alone; the run trains noticeably slower per token and the team blames the larger batch rather than the untouched learning rate.","Quoting batch size in sequences when sequence length varies between experiments; two runs with \"batch 64\" at 2k and 8k context see 4× different numbers of tokens per gradient.","Sizing a schedule in steps, then changing the batch, so warmup and decay now cover a different fraction of the data than intended.","Raising the batch to push GPU utilization and watching loss-per-token flatten; the symptom of exceeding the noise scale is more tokens for the same loss, visible when runs are plotted against tokens rather than steps.","Assuming batch size does not matter for fine-tuning because the dataset is small; with a few thousand examples, a batch of 128 may give only a few dozen optimizer steps per epoch, too few for the optimizer to do anything."],"when":"Treat batch size as the first number fixed in a training plan, because the learning rate, warmup and step\ncount all follow from it. For fine-tuning, start small enough to get at least a few hundred optimizer\nsteps over the run, then raise the micro-batch until the GPU is busy and use accumulation only to reach a\ntarget effective batch. For pre-training or large-scale continued training, pick the effective batch from\nthe model's expected noise scale (prior runs of similar size are the best guide), consider ramping it,\nand scale the learning rate when the batch changes. Always compare runs on loss versus tokens, not loss\nversus steps. In interviews, the depth expected is the noise-scale argument, the coupling with learning\nrate, and the distinction between micro-batch, effective batch and tokens per step.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the micro-batch, accumulation and world-size arithmetic into the config, logs tokens per step, and re-tunes the learning rate whenever the effective batch changes.","architect":"Decides how training throughput is scaled across the fleet (more GPUs at fixed effective batch versus larger batches) with the noise-scale ceiling in mind, because it bounds useful parallelism.","fde":"Translates a customer's hardware constraint into an effective-batch plan their fine-tuning job can actually run, and explains why their small dataset needs a smaller batch than the vendor default."},"check":{"q":"A team moves a pre-training job from 8 GPUs to 64 GPUs by keeping the per-GPU micro-batch and\ndropping gradient accumulation from 8 to 1, so the effective batch is unchanged. Throughput rises 8× but\na colleague proposes \"since we have 8× the GPUs we should also use an 8× larger batch and finish faster\".\nHow do you evaluate that?","a":"The first move is sound: the effective batch, learning rate and schedule are unchanged, so the\noptimization problem is identical and the run simply finishes sooner. The proposal is a different\nexperiment. Multiplying the batch by 8 only shortens the run if the current batch is well below the\ngradient noise scale; if it is near or above it, the step count barely falls and the run consumes up to\n8× more tokens for the same loss. The honest evaluation is to estimate the noise scale from gradient\nstatistics or from prior runs of this model size, scale the learning rate (square-root for Adam as a\nstarting point, then re-tune), lengthen warmup, and compare both configurations on loss versus tokens.\nIf the data budget is fixed, exceeding the noise scale also means fewer effective passes over unique\ndata, which is a quality cost, not just a compute one."},"quiz":[{"q":"What determines the point beyond which increasing batch size stops reducing the number of training steps needed?","options":["GPU memory capacity","The number of layers in the model","The gradient noise scale, the batch size at which the gradient estimate's noise becomes comparable to its signal","The learning-rate schedule's total step count"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once the mini-batch gradient is already an accurate estimate, more samples do not change the step, so steps stop falling while tokens consumed keep rising. Memory limits the micro-batch, not the useful batch."},{"q":"A fine-tuning run doubles its effective batch size and keeps everything else fixed. What is the most likely immediate effect?","options":["Training loss per token improves because gradients are more accurate","Progress per token slows, because the learning rate is now too small for the less noisy gradient","Memory per GPU doubles even if the micro-batch is unchanged","The warmup period becomes twice as long in tokens, improving stability"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A larger batch justifies a larger step; holding the learning rate fixed means each step is as cautious as before but there are half as many of them. Per-GPU memory depends on the micro-batch, not the effective batch."},{"q":"Which change increases the effective batch size without changing per-GPU memory or the time per optimizer step?","options":["Enabling gradient checkpointing","Switching from fp32 to bf16","Adding data-parallel GPUs while holding the per-GPU micro-batch constant","Doubling the sequence length"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each data-parallel rank contributes its micro-batch to the effective batch, so more ranks mean a larger batch per step at the same per-GPU footprint. Checkpointing and bf16 change what fits, not the batch itself."}]},{"key":"ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization","sources":["Srivastava et al., 2014 — Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting","Loshchilov & Hutter, 2019 — Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization","Gal & Ghahramani, 2016 — Dropout as a Bayesian Approximation"],"why":"A network with more parameters than training examples can reach zero training loss by memorizing:\nit learns detectors that fire on accidents of the training set rather than on structure that recurs\nin new data. The visible symptom is the gap between training and validation loss widening as\ntraining continues. The naive fix is to shrink the model until it cannot memorize, but that throws\naway capacity you need for the hard parts of the task, and the \"right\" size is unknowable in\nadvance. Regularization lets you keep the capacity and instead change *what kind* of solution the\noptimizer prefers.\n\nTwo ideas cover most of it. First, penalize complexity directly: add λ·‖w‖² (L2) or λ·‖w‖₁ (L1) to\nthe loss, so large weights must earn their keep by reducing the data loss by more than they cost.\nSecond, inject noise so the training problem is harder than the test problem: corrupt the inputs\n(data augmentation), the labels (label smoothing) or the hidden units (dropout). The noise forces the\nnetwork to find features that are robust to perturbation, and robust features are exactly the ones\nthat transfer to unseen data.\n\nDropout exists because the obvious noise, Gaussian jitter on activations, does not stop\n*co-adaptation*: a unit can still learn to fix up the specific mistakes of its neighbours, and that\nfragile arrangement only works on the training distribution. Randomly deleting units breaks those\nalliances, because a unit cannot depend on a partner that is absent 10–50% of the time.","how":"### The dropout mechanism\nDuring a training forward pass, each activation *x* gets an independent Bernoulli mask\n*m* ~ Bernoulli(1−p), and the layer outputs\n\n```\ny = m ⊙ x / (1 − p)\n```\n\nThe division is \"inverted dropout\", the form every modern framework uses. It is there *because*\nE[y] = (1−p)·x/(1−p) = x: the expected activation is unchanged, so at inference you can run the full\nnetwork with no mask and no rescaling and the next layer sees inputs on the same scale it trained on.\nThe original paper instead kept activations unscaled during training and multiplied the weights by\n(1−p) at test time; the two are equivalent, but inverted dropout keeps inference code untouched.\n\nBackward: the gradient flows only through the kept units (the mask is a constant for that step), so\neach step updates a random sub-network. A network with *n* droppable units contains 2ⁿ such\nsub-networks sharing weights. The inference-time full network is a cheap approximation to averaging\nall of their predictions, which is the ensemble interpretation of why dropout generalizes.\n\n### Where it sits in a transformer\nDropout appears in three places in the original Transformer and its descendants, with one rate\n(0.1 in the base model) shared by all: on the token embeddings, on the attention probabilities after\nthe softmax (\"attention dropout\", which randomly removes some tokens from each head's view), and on\neach sub-layer's output just before it is added to the residual stream (\"residual dropout\"). Because\nthe residual path itself is never dropped, information always has a clean route forward; dropout\nonly perturbs the *corrections* each block adds.\n\n### Weight decay, and why AdamW is not \"Adam plus L2\"\nL2 regularization adds (λ/2)‖w‖² to the loss, whose gradient is λw. With plain SGD the update becomes\nw ← w − η(∇L + λw) = (1 − ηλ)w − η∇L: every step shrinks the weights by a constant factor, which is\nwhy it is also called weight decay. With Adam the two are *not* the same: Adam divides each\nparameter's gradient by a running estimate of its gradient magnitude, so if you put λw into the\ngradient, parameters with large historical gradients get their penalty divided away and are barely\nregularized. AdamW fixes this by applying the shrinkage (1 − ηλ)w *outside* the adaptive step, which\nis what \"decoupled\" means. Standard practice is to exclude biases and LayerNorm gains from decay,\nbecause shrinking a scale parameter toward zero changes what the layer computes rather than how\ncomplex it is.\n\n### Why the data regime decides\nRegularization trades fit for generalization, so it only pays when the model can overfit. A\nfine-tuning run that sees 5,000 examples for three epochs can memorize them; dropout and weight decay\nare doing real work there. A pretraining run that streams trillions of tokens and sees each roughly\nonce cannot memorize in the same way, so dropout mostly costs capacity and slows convergence. That is\nwhy several large-scale pretraining recipes report training with no dropout at all, while\nparameter-efficient fine-tuning libraries still expose a `lora_dropout` knob. Other regularizers\nfollow the same logic: early stopping, label smoothing, stochastic depth and data augmentation all\nbuy the most when data is scarce relative to parameters.","example":"The first block shows inverted dropout preserving the expectation and the train/eval switch. The\nsecond shows the two dropout sites in a transformer block and the standard AdamW parameter split.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn\n\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\np = 0.3\nx = torch.ones(1_000_000)\nmask = (torch.rand_like(x) > p).float()            # keep each unit with probability 1-p\ny_train = x * mask / (1 - p)                       # inverted dropout rescales the survivors\nprint(mask.mean().item(), y_train.mean().item())   # ~0.70 kept, mean ~1.00: expectation preserved\n\ndrop = nn.Dropout(p)\ndrop.train(); print(drop(x)[:6])                   # zeros and 1/(1-p) = 1.4286\ndrop.eval();  print(drop(x)[:6])                   # identity: inference is deterministic\n\nclass Block(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d, p=0.1):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.ln = nn.LayerNorm(d)\n        self.attn = nn.MultiheadAttention(d, 8, dropout=p, batch_first=True)  # attention dropout\n        self.drop = nn.Dropout(p)                                              # residual dropout\n    def forward(self, h):\n        z = self.ln(h)\n        a, _ = self.attn(z, z, z, need_weights=False)\n        return h + self.drop(a)                    # drop the correction, never the residual stream\n\nmodel = Block(64)\ndecay, no_decay = [], []\nfor name, param in model.named_parameters():       # no decay on biases or LayerNorm gains\n    (no_decay if param.ndim < 2 else decay).append(param)\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW([{\"params\": decay, \"weight_decay\": 0.1},\n                         {\"params\": no_decay, \"weight_decay\": 0.0}], lr=3e-4)\n```","tradeoffs":["Dropout lowers the train/validation gap but slows convergence, because each step trains a thinner network with noisier gradients; with abundant data the gap was small anyway and you pay only the slowdown.","Weight decay improves generalization and keeps weights in a range where mixed precision is stable, but too much under-fits; the right λ falls as the dataset grows relative to the model.","Keeping dropout active at inference (Monte Carlo dropout) gives a cheap uncertainty estimate from the spread of several passes, at the cost of several forward passes and non-deterministic outputs.","Label smoothing softens over-confident targets and often helps accuracy, but it distorts the probabilities the model emits, so a calibration step is needed before using them downstream.","Early stopping is the cheapest regularizer, but it ties your model to the validation split; if that split leaks or drifts, you stop at the wrong point."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting `model.eval()` before validation or serving, so dropout stays on; the symptom is noisy, non-reproducible predictions and a validation loss worse than it should be.","Reading training loss computed under dropout as a measure of fit; it is systematically pessimistic, which is why validation loss sometimes looks *lower* than training loss early on.","Passing `weight_decay` to `torch.optim.Adam` and expecting AdamW behaviour; Adam implements it as L2 in the gradient, so heavily-updated weights are barely regularized and results differ.","Applying weight decay to LayerNorm gains and biases; the symptom is a small, persistent accuracy loss that disappears when those parameters are moved to the no-decay group.","Adding dropout to a model that is under-fitting; training and validation loss are both high and close together, and more noise only widens the capacity shortfall.","Placing dropout immediately before a BatchNorm layer; the variance of the activations differs between train and eval mode, so BatchNorm's running statistics are wrong at inference."],"when":"Start from the diagnosis, not the technique. If training loss keeps falling while validation loss\nrises, you are overfitting and regularization is the lever: for fine-tuning, that means weight decay\nthrough AdamW, dropout (including `lora_dropout` for adapters), fewer epochs and early stopping on a\nclean held-out split. If both losses are high and close, you are under-fitting; remove regularization\nand add capacity or data instead. For pretraining-scale data, follow the recipe of the model family\nyou are reproducing, which usually means dropout 0 and modest weight decay. Measure the train/val\ngap per epoch and the validation metric at the eval-mode checkpoint, never the train-mode loss. In\ninterviews, the expected depth is: derive the 1/(1−p) scaling, explain why AdamW differs from Adam\nwith L2, and argue from the data regime rather than reciting default rates.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets dropout and weight-decay groups in the training config, guards every evaluation path with `model.eval()`, and reads overfitting from the train/val curves before touching hyperparameters.","architect":"Decides the fine-tuning data budget and epoch policy that make regularization necessary, and insists validation splits are clean enough that early stopping is trustworthy.","fde":"Explains why a customer's 300-example fine-tune memorized their examples, and reframes the fix around more varied data and a held-out set rather than exotic regularizers."},"check":{"q":"The pretraining team trained your base model with no dropout. The fine-tuning team wants\ndropout 0.1 and weight decay 0.1 for a LoRA run on 4,000 support tickets over three epochs. Are both\nteams right, and what would you check during the run?","a":"Both can be right because the regimes differ. Pretraining streams each token roughly once, so\nthe model cannot memorize individual examples; dropout would only slow convergence and remove\ncapacity, which is why several large-scale recipes omit it. The fine-tune sees each ticket three\ntimes, and a 7B model can memorize 4,000 examples easily, so LoRA dropout, decoupled weight decay and\nearly stopping are appropriate. During the run, compare eval-mode validation loss with training loss\nper epoch: a widening gap means stop earlier or regularize harder; two high, close losses mean the\nregularization is too strong or the adapter rank is too small. Confirm decay excludes biases and\nnorm gains, and that validation tickets do not duplicate training ones, or early stopping will\ntrigger on a leak."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does inverted dropout divide the kept activations by 1 − p during training?","options":["To compensate for the smaller number of gradient updates each unit receives","So the expected activation equals the un-dropped value, letting inference run the full network unchanged","To keep the dropout noise on the same scale as the weight-decay penalty","Because the dropped units are re-inserted with scaled weights at inference"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With probability 1 − p a unit survives, so without rescaling the next layer would see inputs shrunk by that factor in training but not at inference. Scaling by 1/(1 − p) makes the expectation match, which is what makes the deterministic full network a valid inference-time approximation."},{"q":"Why does AdamW apply weight decay separately from the gradient instead of adding λw to it?","options":["Because the L2 penalty is not differentiable at zero and breaks Adam's second-moment estimate","Because decoupling lets the learning rate and the decay rate share a single scheduler","Because Adam divides gradients by a per-parameter magnitude estimate, so an in-gradient penalty is scaled away for frequently updated weights","Because decoupled decay regularizes biases and normalization gains more evenly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Adam normalizes each parameter's update by its running gradient magnitude. If λw is part of that gradient, weights with large gradients get a proportionally tiny penalty. Applying (1 − ηλ)w outside the adaptive step shrinks every weight uniformly, which is the behaviour L2 has under SGD."},{"q":"Which observation most strongly suggests that dropout is hurting a fine-tuning run rather than helping?","options":["Validation loss is slightly lower than training loss during the first epoch","Training loss falls steadily while validation loss starts rising after epoch two","Training and validation loss are both high and stay close together throughout training","Validation accuracy differs by a fraction of a point between two random seeds"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Two high, close losses are under-fitting: the model lacks effective capacity, and dropout removes more. A lower validation loss is a normal artifact of measuring training loss with dropout on, and a rising validation loss is overfitting, the case where dropout helps."}]},{"key":"ml-fundamentals/train-test-leakage","sources":["Kaufman, Rosset, Perlich & Stitelman, 2012 — Leakage in Data Mining: Formulation, Detection, and Avoidance","Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, 2009 — The Elements of Statistical Learning","Kapoor & Narayanan, 2023 — Leakage and the Reproducibility Crisis in Machine-Learning-Based Science","Official docs: scikit-learn (common pitfalls and recommended practices)"],"why":"The whole point of a held-out set is to simulate the future: data the model has never seen, scored\nonce, so the number you get is an unbiased estimate of what will happen after deployment. Every\nleakage mechanism breaks that simulation in a way that flatters the model. Because the error is\nalways in the same direction, it does not average out, and because the model is genuinely good at\nexploiting the leaked signal, nothing in the training loop complains. The loss goes down, the metric\ngoes up, the demo works.\n\nThe naive defence, \"we split the data, so we are fine\", fails because splitting rows is not the same\nas splitting *information*. Information crosses the split through three doors. Through the\n**features**, when a column encodes the label or was recorded after the outcome (a \"cancellation\nreason\" field on a churn dataset is populated only for churners). Through **shared entities and\ntime**, when the same customer, patient or document appears on both sides, or when the model trains\non April to predict March. And through the **fitting procedure**, when any statistic is computed on\nthe full dataset before the split: a scaler's mean, an imputer's median, a target encoder's per-category\naverage, the features chosen by a selection step, or, in LLM work, the prompt wording iterated against\nthe test set. Each door lets the test set shape the model, and the model duly scores well on it.\n\nThe reason this matters more for AI engineers than it did a decade ago is that the leaks have moved\nup a level. Benchmarks sit inside pretraining corpora, golden sets get pasted into few-shot prompts,\nand evaluation sets are regenerated by the model being evaluated. The mechanism is identical; only\nthe surface changed.","how":"### Target leakage: the feature is the label in disguise\nA feature leaks the target when its value is caused by the outcome rather than predictive of it, or\nwhen it is captured after the moment at which a real prediction would be made. The test is a\ntimestamp question: *at the instant the model would run in production, would this value exist with\nthis content?* Columns like \"days until account closed\", \"number of collection calls\" or a support\nticket's \"resolution notes\" fail that test. They are the strongest features in the model *because*\nthey are downstream of the label, and they vanish or change meaning at inference time.\n\n### Row leakage: the split is on the wrong key\nRandom row splits assume rows are independent. They are not when one entity generates many rows\n(several visits per patient, several chunks per document, several turns per conversation) or when\nrows are ordered in time. A model that saw patient 17's other visits in training is partly\nmemorizing patient 17; a model that trained on next quarter's prices is reading the future. The fix\nis to split on the unit of independence: `GroupKFold` by entity id, or a time-ordered split where\nvalidation strictly follows training. The gap between random-split and group- or time-split scores\nis itself a measurement of how much you were leaking.\n\n### Preprocessing leakage: fitted on everything, evaluated on part\nAnything with a `fit` step learns from the rows it sees. Fit a scaler on all rows, then cross-validate,\nand each fold's model trained on inputs normalized with statistics that include the fold's test rows.\nFor a scaler the effect is small; for feature selection or target encoding it is catastrophic, because\nthe selection step has already looked at the test labels to decide which columns survive. The rule\nthat prevents this is mechanical: every fitted transform lives inside the pipeline that is fitted\n*within* each fold, on that fold's training rows only. Resampling (SMOTE, oversampling) belongs to the\nsame category: synthetic minority examples made from the full set put near-copies of test rows into\ntraining.\n\n### Selection leakage: the test set as a training signal\nA single held-out set survives exactly one honest use. Every time you look at the test score and\nchange something (a hyperparameter, a feature, a prompt, a retrieval setting), the test set has voted\non your model and is now a weak training set. After thirty iterations the \"test\" number is an\noptimistically biased validation number. The defence is structural: iterate on a validation split,\nkeep a frozen test split that is evaluated rarely and logged each time, and use nested\ncross-validation when data is too small to afford three partitions.\n\n### LLM-era variants of the same thing\n- **Benchmark contamination:** public eval sets appear in web-scale pretraining data; a high score may\n  be recall, not reasoning. n-gram overlap checks between eval items and training data are the\n  standard, imperfect detector (see the evals module on dataset contamination).\n- **Golden-set bleed:** examples from the eval set appear in the system prompt as few-shots, in the\n  fine-tuning data, or in the retrieval corpus verbatim, so the system is tested on its own notes.\n- **Self-generated evals:** questions written by the model under test, from the chunks the retriever\n  indexes, in the chunk's own vocabulary. Retrieval looks excellent because lexical overlap is high;\n  real users do not phrase questions that way.\n- **Prompt overfitting:** iterating the prompt against the full golden set is selection leakage with a\n  text box instead of a hyperparameter grid.\n\n### Detection checklist\nSuspect leakage when a metric beats the published state of the art or human agreement on first try;\nwhen one feature dominates importance; when random-split and time-split results diverge; when a\nmodel trained on noise still scores above chance; and when production metrics fall far below offline\nones with no distribution shift to blame. Then audit provenance feature by feature: where was this\ncolumn computed, from what snapshot, at what time relative to the label?","example":"The classic demonstration: 100 rows, 10,000 pure-noise features, labels unrelated to anything. Doing\nfeature selection on the full dataset before cross-validating produces impressive accuracy on data\nthat contains no signal at all, because the selector peeked at every fold's test labels. Putting the\nselector inside the pipeline, so it is refitted on each fold's training rows, returns the metric to\nchance where it belongs.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest, f_classif\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold, GroupKFold, cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nX = rng.normal(size=(100, 10_000))                     # pure noise\ny = rng.integers(0, 2, size=100)                       # labels independent of X\ncv = StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=0)\nclf = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)\n\n# WRONG: choose the 20 \"best\" features using all 100 rows, then cross-validate\nX_sel = SelectKBest(f_classif, k=20).fit_transform(X, y)\nleaky = cross_val_score(clf, X_sel, y, cv=cv).mean()\n\n# RIGHT: selection is a pipeline step, refitted on each fold's training rows only\npipe = make_pipeline(SelectKBest(f_classif, k=20), LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000))\nclean = cross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=cv).mean()\nprint(f\"leaky CV accuracy: {leaky:.2f}   clean CV accuracy: {clean:.2f}\")\n# Expect the leaky number far above 0.5 on data with no signal, and the clean one near 0.5.\n\n# Same principle for the split key: if rows share an entity, hold out whole entities\ngroups = rng.integers(0, 20, size=100)                 # e.g. 20 customers, several rows each\ngrouped = cross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=GroupKFold(5), groups=groups).mean()\n```","tradeoffs":["Group- and time-based splits give honest estimates but higher variance and fewer usable folds, which matters when the dataset is small and every held-out entity is expensive.","Freezing a test set and touching it rarely protects the number but slows iteration; teams compensate with a larger validation split and accept a less precise final estimate.","Strict point-in-time feature computation prevents temporal leakage but complicates the data pipeline, which is a large part of why feature stores exist in hybrid ML systems.","Deduplicating near-identical rows across splits removes optimistic bias, but aggressive near-duplicate thresholds can also remove legitimate recurring patterns the model must learn.","Checking eval items against pretraining data for contamination is informative, but n-gram overlap misses paraphrases and translations, so a clean result is weaker evidence than a dirty one."],"pitfalls":["Fitting scalers, imputers, encoders or feature selection before the split; the symptom is cross-validation scores that are higher than the score on a truly fresh batch of data.","Random row splits on data with repeated entities or a time axis; the symptom is a large drop when you rerun with `GroupKFold` or a time-ordered split.","A single feature with implausibly high importance; the symptom is that removing it drops the metric to a mundane level, and its provenance turns out to be post-outcome.","Tuning prompts, retrieval settings or thresholds against the same set you report; the symptom is an offline number that improves every sprint while user-reported quality does not.","Building the RAG golden set by having the model generate questions from indexed chunks; the symptom is near-perfect retrieval recall offline and frequent retrieval misses on real queries.","Few-shot examples or fine-tuning data that overlap the eval set; the symptom is the model reproducing gold answers verbatim, sometimes including their formatting quirks."],"when":"This is not a technique you adopt but a discipline you apply to every evaluation. Before trusting any\nmetric, ask three questions: what is the unit of independence and did we split on it; which steps\nhave a `fit` and do they all live inside the fold; and how many times has this test set influenced a\ndecision. For LLM systems add: did any eval item appear in a prompt, a fine-tuning file or the\nretrieval corpus, and who wrote the eval questions. Measure the gap between random and\ngroup/time splits, keep a log of test-set evaluations, and dedupe across splits with both exact and\nnear-duplicate checks. In interviews, the strong answer to \"your model has 0.99 AUC, what do you do\"\nis \"look for the leak before celebrating\", followed by a concrete audit plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Puts every fitted transform inside the cross-validated pipeline, splits by group or time, and deduplicates eval items against prompts and fine-tuning data before reporting a metric.","architect":"Defines the data-partition policy (frozen test set, point-in-time feature rules, entity-level splits) and the lineage needed to prove an eval set never touched training.","fde":"Audits the customer's \"proven\" model or golden set for post-outcome fields and overlap with the corpus, since inherited evaluations are where most inflated numbers come from."},"check":{"q":"A RAG assistant reports 96% answer correctness. The golden set was produced by prompting the\nsame model to generate question–answer pairs from the indexed chunks, and the prompt was then refined\nover thirty runs against that set. What is wrong with the number, and how would you rebuild the\nevaluation?","a":"Two leaks compound. The questions were written by the system under test in the chunks'\nvocabulary, so retrieval enjoys lexical overlap real users will not provide and the answers mirror\nwhat the model already produces; that inflates both retrieval and generation scores. Then thirty\nrounds of prompt iteration against the full set turned the test set into a validation set, so the\n96% is a selection-biased estimate. Rebuild it with questions sourced from real user logs or written\nby subject-matter experts in their own phrasing, with answers verified independently of the model;\nsplit into a development set for prompt iteration and a frozen test set evaluated only at release\npoints, with each evaluation logged; dedupe test questions against few-shot examples and any\nfine-tuning data; and report the test number alongside the number of times it has been consulted."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does fitting a StandardScaler on the full dataset before splitting count as leakage, even though the labels are never used?","options":["Because the scaler changes the label distribution across folds","Because the test rows' statistics shape the transform the model trains on, so the model has indirectly seen the test distribution","Because StandardScaler is not deterministic across folds","It does not count as leakage; only label-dependent steps can leak"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Any fitted step carries information from the rows it saw. For a scaler the bias is usually small, but the same mistake with feature selection or target encoding is severe, so the rule is uniform: every fitted transform is refitted inside each fold on training rows only."},{"q":"A churn model scores 0.99 AUC in cross-validation and 0.70 in production. Its top feature is `days_since_last_login`, computed from a database snapshot taken after the churn label window closed. Which kind of leakage is this?","options":["Group leakage, because the same customers appear in several folds","Preprocessing leakage, because the feature was scaled before the split","Target leakage, because the feature was captured after the outcome and partly encodes it","Selection leakage, because the test set was reused during tuning"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Customers who churned stopped logging in, so a post-window snapshot of inactivity is a consequence of the label, not a predictor available at decision time. The production drop is the feature reverting to its honest, much weaker predictive value."},{"q":"You must forecast next month's demand for each of 400 existing stores from three years of history. Which split gives an honest estimate?","options":["A shuffled random split stratified by store","A time-ordered split where the validation window comes strictly after the training window","GroupKFold holding out whole stores","Leave-one-row-out cross-validation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The deployment question is \"given the past, predict the future for these stores\", so the validation must be the future. A shuffled split lets the model train on months after the ones it is scored on. Holding out whole stores answers a different question: generalizing to new stores."}]}]});
